# Cross-origin isolation, for the whole site.
#
# QEMU runs its CPU on a worker thread and shares memory with it, which a
# browser permits only on a cross-origin isolated page. Isolation is a property
# of a *document*, applied when the server delivers it -- so it cannot be
# granted to a page reached by a client-side navigation, which creates no
# document at all. Scoping these headers to /krsz-vm therefore meant the VM
# worked when its URL was opened directly and failed when it was reached by
# pressing its tab, which is how anyone actually gets there.
#
# The alternative was to make that one tab reload the page. That worked and was
# wrong: it replayed the boot screen, threw away the client-side router's
# advantage, and made one tab behave unlike the other seven for reasons no
# reader could see. Isolating everything costs nothing here and removes the
# special case.
#
# The price of isolation is that every cross-origin subresource must consent by
# sending cross-origin-resource-policy. Two things are loaded from elsewhere and
# both already do: Google's fonts (fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com
# both send it). The chatbot's weights used to come from bucket.krsz.in, which
# does not send it and has nowhere to add it, so they are served from this
# origin instead -- see src/routes/model/[file]/+server.ts.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
